 ## Overview
-A technique to approximate directional shadows and light scattering by computing the extinction of light intensity for voxels using a 3D Summed Area Table (SAT), avoiding the propagation of illumination from outside the volume [S1, unofficial]. It differs from half-angle slicing [14] by handling any light type and multiple sources, and from Ropinski et al.'s method by computing voxel extinction rather than propagating external illumination [S1, unofficial].
+A technique to approximate directional shadows and light scattering by computing the extinction of light intensity for voxels using a 3D Summed Area Table (SAT), avoiding the propagation of illumination from outside the volume [S1, unofficial]. It differs from half-angle slicing by handling any light type and multiple sources, and from Ropinski et al.'s method by computing voxel extinction rather than propagating external illumination [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-**Inputs:** Scalar volume $V$, transfer function $TF$, light source positions $\mathbf{L}_j$, cone angle $\Psi$, sampling frequency $f$, growth rate $g$, weighting function $h$.
-**Outputs:** Light cache texture containing shadow and ambient occlusion (AO)/color bleeding terms.
